1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:03,810
A go/no-go meeting is a meeting that's held to make

2
00:00:03,810 --> 00:00:07,635
a decision about whether to proceed with a project or not.

3
00:00:07,635 --> 00:00:09,570
In this specific context,

4
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:13,155
the decision is about whether or not to launch a product.

5
00:00:13,155 --> 00:00:15,420
The meeting is typically held towards the end of

6
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:18,510
product development and is typically attended

7
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:20,520
by members of the team as well as

8
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:24,090
stakeholders who have a vested interests in the product success.

9
00:00:24,090 --> 00:00:27,030
This can sometimes include the support and marketing teams

10
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:30,180
too since the launch will impact their worlds.

11
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:33,810
During the meeting the team will present the current state of

12
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:38,340
the product in highlighting any risks or issues.

13
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:43,505
Stakeholders will provide feedback and make a decision about whether to launch or not.

14
00:00:43,505 --> 00:00:45,080
At the end of the meeting,

15
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:48,530
everyone will walk away with the same understanding of the state of

16
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:52,460
the product along with any issues or risks.

17
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:56,360
The outcome of the meeting is typically a clear decision to

18
00:00:56,360 --> 00:01:00,740
either launch the product or not launch the product in its current state.

19
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:03,665
I've seen this happen in three different ways.

20
00:01:03,665 --> 00:01:06,170
The first outcome is a clear yes,

21
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:12,230
let's launch it, but another possible outcome is to launch conditionally.

22
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:16,610
An example this could be you need to address the feedback from the meeting,

23
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:19,880
but once you've addressed that feedback you can go ahead and

24
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:24,165
launch and the last outcome is to not launch.

25
00:01:24,165 --> 00:01:28,510
In this case you'll still have feedback from the meeting that you need to address,

26
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:33,920
but you'll need to come back and run another go or no-go meetings before you can launch.

27
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:36,900
To run a successful go/no-go meeting,

28
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,490
here are a couple of steps that you can take.

29
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:42,200
It's important to invite the right people,

30
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,560
make sure that you plan a clear agenda,

31
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:47,260
run the meeting, make a decision,

32
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:50,285
and follow up on any feedback or action items.

33
00:01:50,285 --> 00:01:53,410
First, let's talk about inviting the right people.

34
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:56,710
The people who attend the go/no-go meetings should be those who have

35
00:01:56,710 --> 00:02:00,805
a vested interest in the product and can provide valuable input.

36
00:02:00,805 --> 00:02:03,960
This may include team members and stakeholders.

37
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:07,265
Next, let's talk about planning the agenda.

38
00:02:07,265 --> 00:02:11,135
The agenda should include an overview of the project,

39
00:02:11,135 --> 00:02:14,465
a review of the progress that's been made so far,

40
00:02:14,465 --> 00:02:18,815
and a discussion of any potential risks or challenges.

41
00:02:18,815 --> 00:02:22,355
It's common that our product manager will facilitate the meeting,

42
00:02:22,355 --> 00:02:26,405
but team members will speak about their own areas of expertise.

43
00:02:26,405 --> 00:02:30,200
For example, a UX designer might talk about the state of

44
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:35,410
the UX and someone from the Q&A team can present the results from testing.

45
00:02:35,410 --> 00:02:38,734
It should also include a section for discussion

46
00:02:38,734 --> 00:02:41,450
and decision-making where attendees can provide

47
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:44,480
their input and make a decision about whether to launch or

48
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:48,270
not then you'll run the meeting.

49
00:02:48,270 --> 00:02:51,440
During the meeting, make sure to stick to

50
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,295
the agenda and give everyone the opportunity to provide their input.

51
00:02:55,295 --> 00:02:59,150
Encourage open and honest discussion and be

52
00:02:59,150 --> 00:03:03,230
prepared to address any concerns or questions that are raised.

53
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:07,280
It's important to discuss the potential risks and challenges in

54
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,150
details and ask for input from all participants.

55
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:14,420
This will help ensure that all perspectives are considered

56
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:18,080
and that any potential risks are thoroughly evaluated.

57
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:21,815
After all of the information has been presented and discussed,

58
00:03:21,815 --> 00:03:23,795
it's time to make a decision.

59
00:03:23,795 --> 00:03:28,215
The group should come to a consensus about whether to launch or not.

60
00:03:28,215 --> 00:03:31,510
This can sometimes be a stressful situation because

61
00:03:31,510 --> 00:03:35,930
so many people have spent a lot of time and energy to build the product,

62
00:03:35,930 --> 00:03:40,090
but remember that sometimes the right thing to do for the user is

63
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:44,650
to postpone the launch if the product isn't ready and there are major issues.

64
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:48,130
I remember one time when I was really nervous going into

65
00:03:48,130 --> 00:03:49,900
a launch meeting because there were

66
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:52,675
some pretty serious problems that needed to be addressed,

67
00:03:52,675 --> 00:03:56,170
but I was surprised and relieved when everyone in

68
00:03:56,170 --> 00:04:00,655
the room unanimously agreed that we should not launch in the current state.

69
00:04:00,655 --> 00:04:02,795
The focus wasn't on blame,

70
00:04:02,795 --> 00:04:05,380
instead we identified the things that would

71
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:08,360
need to be addressed before we could launch the product.

72
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,590
After the decision has been made,

73
00:04:10,590 --> 00:04:12,410
it's important to follow up with

74
00:04:12,410 --> 00:04:16,160
the relevant parties to ensure that everything is moving forward as

75
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:18,200
planned or to take

76
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,940
the appropriate next steps to address

77
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:22,825
the blockers that were discussed during the meeting.

78
00:04:22,825 --> 00:04:28,325
Sometimes you'll go through this process and the decision is to not launch.

79
00:04:28,325 --> 00:04:31,250
This outcome can be a frustrating experience

80
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:34,610
especially when you personally feel like the product is launch-ready.

81
00:04:34,610 --> 00:04:37,565
This does happen and it's okay.

82
00:04:37,565 --> 00:04:41,914
Try to keep a positive attitude and keep the team motivated.

83
00:04:41,914 --> 00:04:45,980
These cases not launching means that the product wasn't ready

84
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:50,045
or would have resulted in a lackluster experience for users due to bugs,

85
00:04:50,045 --> 00:04:51,215
or other issues,

86
00:04:51,215 --> 00:04:53,965
or created too much risks for the business.

87
00:04:53,965 --> 00:04:58,490
For example, if not all the legal or privacy requirements are fully met,

88
00:04:58,490 --> 00:05:00,170
that's a big problem.

89
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:05,120
Launching something when it's not ready is also not a fun experience and can create

90
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,730
a different set of problems and

91
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:11,135
this meeting is something that would prevent that from happening.

92
00:05:11,135 --> 00:05:14,360
Your role as product manager is to work with

93
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:17,510
the team to put together a plan on how to address

94
00:05:17,510 --> 00:05:19,990
the launch blockers that came up during the meeting

95
00:05:19,990 --> 00:05:25,050
including a timeline for when all the issues can be fixed.

